= Context (linguistics) — Technical =

== Context as parameter structure ==
Indexical semantics may model context c as a tuple such as:

<pre>
c = ⟨speaker, addressee, time, place, world, ...⟩
</pre>

A context-sensitive expression can then map a context to content or semantic value.

The exact parameters depend on the theory.

== Dynamic context ==
Dynamic semantics treats interpretation as context change.

A sentence may be modeled as transforming an information state:

<pre>
C  ──utterance──▶  C'
</pre>

This supports analyses of anaphora, presupposition, discourse reference, and update.

== Discourse and social context ==
Broader pragmatic models include:

* discourse commitments;
* Questions Under Discussion;
* common ground;
* participant roles;
* communicative goals;
* perceptual joint attention;
* institutional and cultural constraints.

No finite formal tuple automatically captures every socially relevant circumstance.

== Context selection problem ==
If “context” includes everything surrounding an utterance, it loses explanatory force.

A theory therefore needs a relevance or selection principle specifying which variables affect a given interpretive process.

== Context versus common ground ==
Possible-world common-ground models encode mutually accepted propositions; indexical contexts encode utterance parameters; discourse-representation models encode discourse referents and accessibility. They solve different problems and should not be collapsed.

